<Routines>
<Routine Name="MainRoutine" Type="RLL">
<RLLContent>
<Rung Number="0" Type="N">
<Comment>
<![CDATA[



All information provided  -- No warranty or implied merchantability.
Refer to the RSLogix 5000 End User License Agreement (EULA) in the Release Notes.
 
  
 
 

]]>
</Comment>
<Text>
<![CDATA[NOP();]]>
</Text>
</Rung>
<Rung Number="1" Type="N">
<Comment>
<![CDATA[==================================================================
Sample program for Kinematics with orientation feature set (MCTO)
with Cartesian coordinate_system=Cartesian (6 Virtual axes, Geometry=Cartesian)
and  Robot coordinate_system=Delta5D (5 Joint axes, Geometry=Delta J1J2J3J4J5)   
====   ==== ==== ==== ==== ==== ====
Using  Coordinated path moves (MCPM) to demonstrate 
how to program the motion of the Ry orientation axis beyond
the +/- 90 degree mirror image boundaries.  See the read_me program for more info.
open Trend=P_V_A  to view the Position plot of the sample MCPM moves.   
==================================================================]]>
</Comment>
<Text>
<![CDATA[XIC(S:FS)MOV(-1,pathMoveNumber)OTU(demonstrateLongestRotary);]]>
</Text>
</Rung>
<Rung Number="2" Type="N">
<Comment>
<![CDATA[MCTO must be active for mirror image axis behavior]]>
</Comment>
<Text>
<![CDATA[EQU(-1,pathMoveNumber)XIC(Motion_group.GroupSynced)[MCTO(Cartesian,Delta_J1J2J3J4J5,mctoStatus[0],0,0) ,XIC(mctoStatus[0].IP) MOV(0,pathMoveNumber) ];]]>
</Text>
</Rung>
<Rung Number="3" Type="N">
<Comment>
<![CDATA[move to starting position Rx=0.0°, Ry=(-78°), Rz=180.0°
]]>
</Comment>
<Text>
<![CDATA[EQU(0,pathMoveNumber)[MCPM(Cartesian,mcpmStatus[0],path[0],1,pathDynamics,0,0) ,XIC(mcpmStatus[0].PC) [XIO(demonstrateLongestRotary) MOV(1,pathMoveNumber) ,XIC(demonstrateLongestRotary) MOV(4,pathMoveNumber) ] ];]]>
</Text>
</Rung>
<Rung Number="4" Type="N">
<Comment>
<![CDATA[Example #1: move to mirror non-flip orientation Rx=180, Ry=(-45), Rz=0
This orientation move crosses the -90 degree boundary, moving in the positive direction.
Note that the axis position counts down to -90, then back up to the target angle -45°

NOTE: This program does not cause motion on Rz.  Different values of programmed Rz reflect the effects of mirror flip orientation caused by Ry  mirror boundary crossing.]]>
</Comment>
<Text>
<![CDATA[EQU(1,pathMoveNumber)[MCPM(Cartesian,mcpmStatus[1],path[1],1,pathDynamics,0,0) ,XIC(mcpmStatus[1].PC) MOV(2,pathMoveNumber) ];]]>
</Text>
</Rung>
<Rung Number="5" Type="N">
<Comment>
<![CDATA[Example #2: move to non-flip orientation Rx=180, Ry = 45, Rz = 0
This orientation move is 90 degrees in the positive direction.  No mirror boundary is crossed.]]>
</Comment>
<Text>
<![CDATA[EQU(2,pathMoveNumber)[MCPM(Cartesian,mcpmStatus[2],path[2],1,pathDynamics,0,0) ,XIC(mcpmStatus[2].PC) MOV(3,pathMoveNumber) ];]]>
</Text>
</Rung>
<Rung Number="6" Type="N">
<Comment>
<![CDATA[Example #3: move to flip orientation Rx=0, Ry=90, Rz=180
This orientation move places Ry at the +90 degree boundary.]]>
</Comment>
<Text>
<![CDATA[EQU(3,pathMoveNumber)[MCPM(Cartesian,mcpmStatus[3],path[3],1,pathDynamics,0,0) ,XIC(mcpmStatus[3].PC) OTL(demonstrateLongestRotary) MOV(0,pathMoveNumber) ];]]>
</Text>
</Rung>
<Rung Number="7" Type="N">
<Comment>
<![CDATA[Example #4: move to flip orientation Rx=0, Ry=78, Rz=180
This orientation move demonstrates the MCPM longest rotary path where start and end point are both mirror flip orientation, one positive and the other negative.
]]>
</Comment>
<Text>
<![CDATA[EQU(4,pathMoveNumber)[MCPM(Cartesian,mcpmStatus[4],path[4],1,pathDynamics,0,0) ,XIC(mcpmStatus[4].PC) MOV(5,pathMoveNumber) ];]]>
</Text>
</Rung>
</RLLContent>
</Routine>
<Routine Name="read_me" Type="ST">
<STContent>
<Line Number="0">
<![CDATA[]]>
</Line>
<Line Number="1">
<![CDATA[(*]]>
</Line>
<Line Number="2">
<![CDATA[ /*  ============================================================================= */	]]>
</Line>
<Line Number="3">
<![CDATA[				   Using MCPM to Program Ry Absolute Moves ]]>
</Line>
<Line Number="4">
<![CDATA[				   for Geometries with Mirror Image Orientation.]]>
</Line>
<Line Number="5">
<![CDATA[ /*  ============================================================================= */	]]>
</Line>
<Line Number="6">
<![CDATA[         ]]>
</Line>
<Line Number="7">
<![CDATA[]]>
</Line>
<Line Number="8">
<![CDATA[ Download the program to controller.  Program automatically starts on transition to RUN.]]>
</Line>
<Line Number="9">
<![CDATA[ ]]>
</Line>
<Line Number="10">
<![CDATA[ Use the trend window to visualize the Ry mirror image behavior around +/- 90°, noting]]>
</Line>
<Line Number="11">
<![CDATA[ that the corresponding J5 axis position does exceed 90° while Ry does not.]]>
</Line>
<Line Number="12">
<![CDATA[    ]]>
</Line>
<Line Number="13">
<![CDATA[The programmed orientation moves in this example program reflect the Ry moves as shown ]]>
</Line>
<Line Number="14">
<![CDATA[in the diagram accompanying the online help topic regarding the use of MCPM to program ]]>
</Line>
<Line Number="15">
<![CDATA[Ry Absolute moves for geometries with mirror image orientation behavior.]]>
</Line>
<Line Number="16">
<![CDATA[]]>
</Line>
<Line Number="17">
<![CDATA[In that diagram, four examples are shown (all orientation values are absolute):]]>
</Line>
<Line Number="18">
<![CDATA[]]>
</Line>
<Line Number="19">
<![CDATA[Example #1]]>
</Line>
<Line Number="20">
<![CDATA[Starting orientation]]>
</Line>
<Line Number="21">
<![CDATA[Rx = 0° ]]>
</Line>
<Line Number="22">
<![CDATA[Ry = -78° ]]>
</Line>
<Line Number="23">
<![CDATA[Rz = 180°]]>
</Line>
<Line Number="24">
<![CDATA[Ending orientation]]>
</Line>
<Line Number="25">
<![CDATA[Rx = 180°]]>
</Line>
<Line Number="26">
<![CDATA[Ry = -45°]]>
</Line>
<Line Number="27">
<![CDATA[Rz = 0°]]>
</Line>
<Line Number="28">
<![CDATA[This results in positive Ry motion from -78 degrees to the -90 boundary in the mirror ]]>
</Line>
<Line Number="29">
<![CDATA[flip region of orientation, then continues in the mirror non-flip region to stop at -45 ]]>
</Line>
<Line Number="30">
<![CDATA[degrees for a total +57 degrees.  There is no actual motion seen on Rz - the value flip]]>
</Line>
<Line Number="31">
<![CDATA[from 180 to 0 reflects the Rz axis position change between the mirror flip and mirror]]>
</Line>
<Line Number="32">
<![CDATA[non-flip regions of travel.]]>
</Line>
<Line Number="33">
<![CDATA[]]>
</Line>
<Line Number="34">
<![CDATA[Example #2]]>
</Line>
<Line Number="35">
<![CDATA[Starting orientation = Ending orientation of Example #1]]>
</Line>
<Line Number="36">
<![CDATA[Ending orientation]]>
</Line>
<Line Number="37">
<![CDATA[Rx = 180°]]>
</Line>
<Line Number="38">
<![CDATA[Ry = 45°]]>
</Line>
<Line Number="39">
<![CDATA[Rz = 0°]]>
</Line>
<Line Number="40">
<![CDATA[This results in positive Ry motion from -45 to +45, with no mirror boundary crossed and ]]>
</Line>
<Line Number="41">
<![CDATA[all rotation occurring in the mirror non-flip region.]]>
</Line>
<Line Number="42">
<![CDATA[]]>
</Line>
<Line Number="43">
<![CDATA[Example #3]]>
</Line>
<Line Number="44">
<![CDATA[Starting orientation = Ending orientation of Example #2]]>
</Line>
<Line Number="45">
<![CDATA[Ending orientation]]>
</Line>
<Line Number="46">
<![CDATA[Rx = 0°]]>
</Line>
<Line Number="47">
<![CDATA[Ry = 90°]]>
</Line>
<Line Number="48">
<![CDATA[Rz = 180°]]>
</Line>
<Line Number="49">
<![CDATA[This results in positive Ry rotation from 45 degrees and stopping at the 90 degree mirror]]>
</Line>
<Line Number="50">
<![CDATA[flip boundary.  Being on the 90° boundary cross causes a flip on Rx and Rz.]]>
</Line>
<Line Number="51">
<![CDATA[]]>
</Line>
<Line Number="52">
<![CDATA[Example #4]]>
</Line>
<Line Number="53">
<![CDATA[Starting orientation = Starting orientation of Example #1]]>
</Line>
<Line Number="54">
<![CDATA[Rx = 0° ]]>
</Line>
<Line Number="55">
<![CDATA[Ry = -78° ]]>
</Line>
<Line Number="56">
<![CDATA[Rz = 180°]]>
</Line>
<Line Number="57">
<![CDATA[Ending orientation]]>
</Line>
<Line Number="58">
<![CDATA[Rx = 0°]]>
</Line>
<Line Number="59">
<![CDATA[Ry = 78°]]>
</Line>
<Line Number="60">
<![CDATA[Rz = 180°]]>
</Line>
<Line Number="61">
<![CDATA[The resultant move takes the longest rotary path move to avoid travel through 0° in the ]]>
</Line>
<Line Number="62">
<![CDATA[Mirror flip region, or +204° on Ry (-204° for J5).]]>
</Line>
<Line Number="63">
<![CDATA[]]>
</Line>
<Line Number="64">
<![CDATA[]]>
</Line>
<Line Number="65">
<![CDATA[*)]]>
</Line>
